artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let T be an n×n real symmetric tridiagonal matrix, and let a subset of its eigenpairs be desired. The Algorithm of Multiple Relatively Robust Representations (MR^3) computes approximate eigenpairs of T as follows. It maintains a representation tree whose nodes are bidiagonal factorizations LDL^T of shifted matrices T - σI. At the root, an initial relatively robust representation L_0 D_0 L_0^T = T - σ_0 I is formed. Approximate eigenvalues are computed for each representation; eigenvalues are partitioned into singletons (relatively isolated) and clusters (close in a relative sense). For each singleton λ, an approximate eigenvector is computed by a twisted-factorization / RQI-type step from the local representation. For each cluster, a new shift σ' near the cluster is chosen, a new factorization L'D'(L')^T = LDL^T - σ' I is formed via differential stationary qd transforms (dstqds), and the algorithm recurses. All arithmetic is performed in finite-precision floating-point with unit roundoff ε.

Provide a "TCS-style" worst-case analysis of MR^3: that is, exhibit a finite list of conditions on the input matrix T (and on user-specified parameters such as the cluster-separation threshold, the relative-gap tolerance, and the shift selection rule) that
(i) can each be checked, given T and the parameters, in time polynomial in n and in the bit length of the input;
(ii) imply that the algorithm terminates in a number of arithmetic operations bounded by an explicit polynomial in n and the number k of requested eigenpairs;
(iii) imply that the computed eigenvectors v̂_1, …, v̂_k satisfy explicit a priori bounds of the form ‖T v̂_i − λ̂_i v̂_i‖ ≤ p(n) ε ‖T‖ and |v̂_i^T v̂_j| ≤ q(n) ε for i ≠ j, with p and q explicit polynomials.

Equivalently, formulate verifiable sufficient conditions on T alone (e.g., on element growth, relative gaps, and pivot magnitudes encountered in the canonical execution) under which MR^3 is provably correct and convergent, and prove the resulting orthogonality and residual bounds.
